+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+- **Optimization:** In practice, the loop-based mapping can be optimized to a single bitwise XOR operation instead of a loop, as the permutation for each sign combination can be represented as a fixed XOR mask applied to the Morton code [S1].
+- **Precision:** Morton codes can grow large. For `max_level=16`, the code requires $16 \times 3 = 48$ bits, fitting in a standard 64-bit integer. For deeper trees, arbitrary precision or 128-bit integers may be required [S2, unofficial].
+- **Boundary Conditions:** The method assumes the ray direction is not exactly zero. If a component is zero, the sign is typically treated as positive or handled via a specific tie-breaking rule, though the source defines strict `< 0` checks [S1].
